WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:00.780
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Companies looking

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.210
to make predictions require
vast amounts of data,

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.270
complex calculations,
and rapid responses.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.847
Join us as we talk
to LiftIgniter

00:00:07.847 --> 00:00:09.930
about how they built the
machine learning platform

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.417
on this episode of Stack Chat.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:14.980
Thanks for joining us.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.530
Tell us a little bit
more about LiftIgniter

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:17.730
and how you built your platform.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.480
VENKAT VENKATARAMAN:
Thanks for having me.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.900
LiftIgniter is a real-time
personalization platform

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.560
powered by machine learning
models running in the cloud.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.000
By real time, we mean
end-to-end latency

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.730
in the order of about
100 to 150 milliseconds.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.360
And this includes the
network latency as well.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.470
Now, this is a real
challenge both in terms

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.420
of scale and
computation because we

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.190
track a number of
signals by users

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.230
in order to provide them
with the most relevant

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.330
and personalized
recommendations.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.420
Our world view is that
each person is unique.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.030
And at every moment
in time, our platform

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.130
is optimized for the
most relevant results

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.800
based on all the signals we
gather up until that point.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.150
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Can you
show us the architecture

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.400
you built to keep this moving?

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.420
VENKAT VENKATARAMAN: Sure.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.790
As an ML company that
crunches and stores

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:03.450
huge volumes of data,
infrastructure cost

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.760
has a large impact
on our business.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.710
We focused on three
main factors--

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.170
pricing, latency,
and reliability,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.520
just like anyone else.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.910
To emphasize these, we
used three main techniques.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.670
Auto-scaling based on
workload, lets us make sure

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.160
that we have the
flexibility to scale out

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.230
heavy workloads on demand.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.210
And we use a combination
of auto-scale groups

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.420
either for preemptible instances
and for on demand, which

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.910
leads us to the second point,
which is the flexibility

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.670
to allow us to use
preemptive instances as

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.750
needed, and thirdly,
the option to move

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.970
stale but useful data
into cold storage, again,

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.440
for cost reduction.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.990
When it comes to speed,
network latency usually

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.670
has a huge part to play.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.680
But Google's network
has been pretty snappy

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.280
in our experience.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.480
We have been able to keep
components that chatter a lot

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.620
close by within the same
region with enough redundancy.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.620
With Google's Footprint, we're
able to create and run services

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.130
closer to the edge so that all
the customer-facing services

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.582
perform really well.

00:02:03.582 --> 00:02:05.040
And we have also
been able to avoid

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.160
any outages that'll affect our
SLA, thanks to Google Cloud.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.419
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Can you
tell us a little bit more

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:11.280
about how the recommendation
system was actually built?

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:12.390
VENKAT VENKATARAMAN: Sure.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.790
The diagram is actually
a simplified architecture

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:15.810
diagram.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.210
We are not showing the
load balancer, and proxies,

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:19.320
and things like those.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.870
But it just focuses on the main
components and the services

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.910
that we have in
our architecture.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.970
The requests to our
cloud are either

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.190
going to hit the
front-end APIs, or what

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.460
we call as the model servers.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.970
The model servers
are the ones which

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.950
for anything that has
got to do with user

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.700
activity and returning
recommendation results,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.860
that's taken care of by
the model servers, where

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.270
all the magic happens.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.300
For everything else,
like, for example,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.400
updating or creating
new inventory items,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.500
or making other API
calls will actually

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.550
hit our front-end services.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.450
Now looking at the front-end
services, when an API

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.820
call comes in, a
bunch of processing

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.950
happens on those
front-end machines.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.740
And the front-end services,
they push the data

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.360
into data storage.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.040
We create either
files or other data,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.040
push it into some data storage
like Google Cloud Storage.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.900
We also have other data stores.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.620
And GCS would actually
trigger an event

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.480
into Cloud Pub/Sub where we have
a lot of subscribers listening

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.500
on messages on those topics.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.412
And a subscriber could pick up
those, do further processing,

00:03:23.412 --> 00:03:24.870
and an example
shown in the diagram

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.060
is where we have an
inventory system which

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.580
does further analysis
and processing based

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:30.720
on what the trigger is.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.520
The model servers are the
ones, like I said before,

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.080
which handle all of
recommendations and the user

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.340
activity tracking.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.110
They do a lot of
processing in-house--

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:42.568
a lot of magic happens here,
a lot of heavy lifting happens

00:03:42.568 --> 00:03:43.110
here--

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.870
pushes the data, once
again, into data storage.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.390
A similar mechanism happens,
which triggers an event

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:49.830
into Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.210
And we have
subscribers listening

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:54.160
on those topics, which
pick the data from GCS,

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:55.740
do further processing.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.630
And as shown in the
diagram, this data

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.450
is now pushed real
time into BigQuery

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:05.040
where our dashboards are fed
off of BigQuery for analytics.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.920
The key thing here is
while we can move things

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.650
into BigQuery in real
time, BigQuery can also

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:15.400
get a little expensive, so we
want to keep access to BigQuery

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.871
only when we really need it.

00:04:16.871 --> 00:04:19.079
So we have an offline
processing which takes data off

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:21.839
of BigQuery, does
further aggregation,

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:24.460
pushes it into Cloud SQL, which
acts like a caching layer.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:27.780
So the dashboards would either
hit Cloud SQL our BigQuery

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.430
depending on how far
back in time they

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:30.760
want to go and gather data.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:34.080
The box at the bottom shows
our Spark cluster, which

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.620
runs off of Cloud Dataproc.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:38.130
And the Spark
cluster is basically

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.010
used for tuning our
models and retraining it

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:42.570
for better accuracy.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:44.440
We run it on a nightly basis.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.740
We gather all the data from GCS.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.360
We have the models
running all over again.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.380
And if the results of those
models with the tune parameters

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.150
are better than
what it was before,

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.755
we make the necessary changes
and push those configuration

00:04:56.755 --> 00:04:57.380
into the cloud.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:58.950
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Now,
it's been a while since you

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.138
architected this originally.

00:05:00.138 --> 00:05:01.680
If you could go back
and do it again,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:02.560
what would you do differently?

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:04.935
VENKAT VENKATARAMAN: We started
architecting our platform

00:05:04.935 --> 00:05:08.680
around 2013, 2014 when machine
learning was still not hot.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.300
Big data was still
the thing of the day.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.530
It wasn't as hard or widely
available as it is today.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.920
If we had all the libraries and
frameworks that are available

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.050
today back then,
then some of the

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:23.570
build versus buy decisions
would have been different.

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:25.330
We also enabled
augmented intelligence

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:28.960
by giving our users the ability
to influence recommendations.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:30.790
Sometimes they may
implement rules

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:32.480
that can get a bit
too restrictive

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.540
and end up returning
less results.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.720
To help enable the
customization for these users,

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:39.550
we've been researching
indexing solutions

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:41.170
to give better results.

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:43.000
Part of this was
investigating something

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.380
like Elasticsearch,
which was good,

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.380
but it wasn't
performing as fast as we

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:50.345
needed because of a really
tight latency requirements.

00:05:50.345 --> 00:05:51.970
We're currently
looking into other ways

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:54.820
to build this and keep serving
improve recommendations.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:56.620
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Thanks so
much for sharing the details.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:57.400
VENKAT VENKATARAMAN:
My pleasure.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:58.690
MARK MIRCHANDANI: If you'd
like to try setting up

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:01.460
Pub/Sub notifications for
Google Cloud Storage yourself,

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.902
check out the walkthrough
in the description below.

00:06:03.902 --> 00:06:05.860
Thanks for watching, and
make sure to subscribe

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:08.230
for more great Google
Cloud Platform content.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.080
We'll see you next
time on Stack Chat.

